#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119586 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection"}] :question/id #uuid "b36a943c-f00a-5436-9256-0725a0008fdb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5410" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form. We see that the matrix above has two pivot columns: the $1$st and the $2$nd columns. This tells us that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}\n$$\n\nforms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of two vectors, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* two vectors. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119587 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "004c4177-ca0e-586c-962a-1220b4b64f47" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span Using Row Reduction"}] :question/id #uuid "ef028d89-e645-5ef3-a881-092292e2a271" :question/math-academy-id "e-5411" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 3 & -15 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + 3R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & R_{3}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{3} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix above has one pivot columns: the $1$st column. This tells us that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{v}_1 \\}\n$$\n\nforms a basis for our span.\n\nSince $\\mathcal{B}$ consists of one vector, any basis of our span will also have *exactly* one vector. Therefore, the dimension of the span is $1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "004c4177-ca0e-586c-962a-1220b4b64f47" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "860066dc-9678-5a9d-bd86-3b1b4c18b1e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118258} {:answer/id #uuid "8ffc6e4a-31a3-509a-b8b1-cf21e52432c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118259} {:answer/id #uuid "9942e06c-9691-5e59-8c96-1e6d1e996cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118260} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0986b8-8d54-5fee-aee8-e21bc2520f0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118261} {:answer/id #uuid "36bf313b-75f8-5376-971e-919c2d325bb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9942e06c-9691-5e59-8c96-1e6d1e996cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118260} :answer-field/id #uuid "01959ec0-9d78-5ae7-8581-d050edc64ee8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118257}] :question/id #uuid "5704a84d-0989-5302-98de-8c50b889e0c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-34854" :question/problem "What is $dim(Span{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}})$?"}]}
